\textit{Effect of pretraining.} To isolate the contribution of pretraining, we compare RAN-NFM-Core with two variants that share its architecture, inputs, and prediction head (Table~\ref{tab:pretrain}). Core-Scratch is trained from random initialization with the same fine-tuning procedure as RAN-NFM-Core, and thus measures the benefit of pretraining as an initialization. Core-Frozen keeps the pretrained backbone fixed and trains only the prediction head and, for NR, the input adapters for the new configuration features, and thus measures how informative the pretrained representations are on their own. For RANPerf, all three variants use the same XGBoost classifier on the exported embeddings as RAN-NFM-Core (Section 4.2).

\textit{RANPerf:} On LTE, pretraining improves AUPRC from [X] to 0.2510 and F1 from [X] to 0.2909. Core-Frozen achieves an AUPRC of [X], whereas the same frozen architecture with random weights reaches only 0.07. The pretrained representations therefore carry task-relevant information before any fine-tuning, while fine-tuning is needed to reach their full benefit. On NR, RAN-NFM-Core achieves a higher AUPRC than Core-Scratch ([X] vs. [X]) but a lower F1 ([X] vs. [X]). F1 is computed at a single decision threshold and therefore reflects only one operating point, whereas AUPRC summarizes precision across all operating points. Core-Scratch achieves high precision at moderate recall, but its precision drops faster as recall increases and falls below that of RAN-NFM-Core beyond about 42\% recall. This is consistent with the limited NR supervision (about [X] positive training samples): a model trained from scratch identifies a subset of positive changes with high confidence but generalizes less well to the rest, whereas the representations pretrained on LTE data keep the precision more stable as recall increases. Core-Frozen achieves an AUPRC of [X] on NR.

\textit{EngSav:} Pretraining reduces the relative MAPE from [X] to 0.940 and the relative RMSE from [X] to 0.965, and lowers the 25th, 50th, and 75th percentile errors from [X], [X], and [X] to 0.924, 0.957, and 0.972, respectively. Core-Frozen attains a relative MAPE of [X].

\textit{EngCon:} Core-Scratch achieves lower errors than RAN-NFM-Core (relative MAPE [X] vs. 0.591). Because weekly eNodeB energy consumption is relatively stable over time and the same eNodeBs appear in both the training and test periods, this task can be learned well without pretraining. Core-Frozen attains a relative MAPE of [X].

Overall, pretraining improves the primary metric (AUPRC for RANPerf and MAPE for EngSav and EngCon) in three of the four settings, with the largest gain on [X].
